{"Narrowing the lock to only the cache-write removes the mutual exclusion that prevented concurrent builds for the same key. Two goroutines calling BuildIndex with identical key will both create and populate a bleve index in parallel. For file-backed indices this calls bleve.New on the same directory twice, which will fail or corrupt. For memory indices the loser's index is silently leaked (never closed). Consider using a per-key lock (e.g., sync.Map of sync.Mutex per key or singleflight) so that concurrent builds of the same key are serialized while different keys can still proceed in parallel.": 0.9471718072891235, "TotalDocs iterates b.cache without acquiring b.cacheMu.RLock(). Since BuildIndex now writes to the map under the lock, any concurrent call to TotalDocs during index building will trigger Go's fatal error: concurrent map iteration and map write runtime panic. Add b.cacheMu.RLock()/b.cacheMu.RUnlock() around the iteration.": 0.7740212082862854, "TODO skips the only integration test for postgres but doesn't reference a tracking issue (e.g., a GitHub issue). Without one, this gap is likely to be forgotten and the test will remain disabled indefinitely. Consider filing an issue and referencing it here: // TODO(#XXXXX) - fix this test for postgres": 0.22751592099666595, "This change disables the integration test for postgres entirely, removing coverage for a supported backend and allowing postgres-specific regressions to slip through.": 0.5632165670394897, "The same trace propagation bug this PR fixes in GetResourceStats still exists in WriteEvent, Read, and List in this file. All three discard the span-enriched context (_, span := ...), so child spans won't be linked to their parent. WriteEvent is especially impactful since it passes the un-enriched ctx down to create/update/delete, which each start their own spans that won't show WriteEvent as parent.": 0.1832101196050644, "Duplicate error log: Init() already logs error initializing resource server internally before returning. Remove the outer log to avoid double-logging the same error.": 0.8496392965316772}